-- title:
--   Residual irreducibility, oddness and inertial unipotence for a congruent newform
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime with $p \neq 2$, and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $\mathbb{Z}$ with $\Delta_W \neq 0$ which is a semistable model, meaning that for every prime $q$ dividing $\Delta_W$ one has $q \nmid c_4(W)$, and whose mod $p$ representation is irreducible in the sense that the $p$-torsion of $W_{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is nontrivial and its only Galois-stable $\mathbb{Z}/p$-submodules are $0$ and the whole group. Let $M, L$ be nonzero naturals with $L \mid M$, let $g$ be a weight two cusp form on $\Gamma_0(L)$ which is a newform (a normalised Hecke eigenform whose eigensystem does not already occur at any proper divisor of $L$), and let $\mathfrak{m}$ be a maximal ideal of the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $\mathbb{C}$ containing $p$. Assume that for every prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid \Delta_W$, $\ell \nmid M$ and $\ell \neq p$ there is an algebraic integer $a$ equal, in $\mathbb{C}$, to the $\ell$-th $q$-expansion coefficient of $g$ and with $a - a_\ell(W) \in \mathfrak{m}$, where $a_\ell(W) = \ell + 1 - \#W(\mathbb{F}_\ell)$ is computed from the reduction of $W$ modulo $\ell$. Then there exist a finite set $S$ of naturals, a ring $O'$ which is a characteristic zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field and with $p$ in its maximal ideal, a ring homomorphism $\chi$ from the Hecke algebra $\mathtt{heckeAlgebra}\ L\ 2\ S$ (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of weight two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(L)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for $\ell$ prime, $\ell \nmid L$, $\ell \notin S$, and $U_q$ for primes $q \mid L$, $q \notin S$) to $\mathbb{C}$, a ring homomorphism $\iota$ from the range of $\chi$ to $O'$, and a two-dimensional adic Galois representation $\rho$ over $O'$ (a free $O'$-module $V$ of rank $2$ with an adically continuous monoid homomorphism from $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ to $\operatorname{End}_{O'} V$) such that: $\chi(T_\ell)$ is the $\ell$-th $q$-expansion coefficient of $g$ for all primes $\ell \nmid L$ with $\ell \notin S$; for each such $\ell$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\ell$ a nonunit of $A$ and every $\sigma$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ acting as $x \mapsto x^{\ell}$ on the residue field satisfy $\operatorname{charpoly}(\rho(\sigma)) = X^2 - \iota(\chi(T_\ell)) X + \ell$; the residual representation of $\rho$ (the base change of $V$ to the residue field of $O'$) has no Galois-stable subspace other than $0$ and everything, and $\det$ of every involution $\neq 1$ acting on it is $-1$; and for every prime $q \neq p$, the residual representation, regarded as an adic representation over the residue field, is unipotent on inertia at $q$, that is, $\operatorname{charpoly}$ of the action of any element of the inertia subgroup of any valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ in which $q$ is a nonunit equals $(X-1)^2$.
--
--   This assembles the $p$-adic representation attached by Eichler–Shimura–Deligne theory to a newform $g$ congruent to a semistable elliptic curve into a package carrying exactly the local and global hypotheses required by the level-lowering argument: prescribed Frobenius characteristic polynomials outside a finite set, residual irreducibility and oddness transported from the curve, and unipotence on inertia away from $p$. It feeds the subsequent analysis of the Steinberg quotient and of the eigensystem on $H^1$ in the Frey curve route to Fermat's Last Theorem.
